  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,1)$ whose second component is $1.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $1.$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,3)$ whose second component is $3.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $3:$ \n\n- Linking $(2,3)$ with $(3,1)$ requires that $(2,1)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(2,3)$ with $(3,2)$ requires that $(2,2)\\in R.$ However, this pair is not in $R.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nWe can stop checking since we have found a chain of pairs that do not satisfy the transitivity condition. Therefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince $(2,3)\\in R$ and $(3,2)\\in R,$ but $(2,2)\\notin R,$ the relation is $\\text{not transitive}.$",
